alt-syscall: allow the syscalls AOSP 17 userspace uses in "android"

Port of project-vmware 027 to rk3588; the hunks are identical. The ARC++
container runs under the "android" alt-syscall table (config.json
"altSyscall": "android"), and every process init starts inherits it.
AOSP 17 userspace calls syscalls the table predates, which then fail with
-ENOSYS. On fydetab_duo (.124, android-container-cb) that shows up as about
400 "blocked syscall 276" (renameat2) lines an hour: bionic's rename() now
calls renameat2 first and falls back to renameat, so nothing breaks, but
UnityMain, usagestats, LazyTaskWriter and gms flood the kernel log.

  * renameat2: bionic's rename()/renameat(), installd's atomic writes and
    netbpfload's RENAME_NOREPLACE pinning.
  * faccessat2, fchmodat2, close_range, copy_file_range, preadv2,
    pwritev2, epoll_pwait2, clone3, rseq, mlock2, mseal: newer variants of
    calls the table already allows, which bionic, ART and libbase now try
    first. 074 and 080 add some of these to the "complete" table only; the
    "android" table had none of them, so nothing here is a duplicate.
  * process_madvise, process_mrelease, pidfd_send_signal, sched_getattr:
    used by lmkd and ActivityManager (compaction, reaping, pidfd kills)
    alongside the already allowed pidfd_open.

Each has the same permission checks as the call it extends, so they go
in the shared 64-bit list (the fydroid images are 64-bit only).
sched_setattr stays out: the table routes sched_setscheduler and
sched_setparam through android_* wrappers that limit priorities, and
sched_setattr would bypass them.

